# izh_2004l_integrator.odex: converted from izh_2004l_integrator.ode by xppautX --convert (docs/odex.md)

# Izhikevich's simple model of a spiking neuron: integrator.
# Izhikevich E.M. (2004) Which model to use for cortical spiking neurons? IEEE Trans. Neural Networks 15(5):1063-1070, Fig. 1, panel L.
#   v' = 0.04 v^2 + 4.1 v + 108 - u + I,  u' = a (b v - u);
#   when v reaches 30 mV, v is set to c and u to u + d (the global event).
# Input: four pulses I0=9 of width w=2: a pair 5 apart (T1, T2) and a pair 10 apart (T3, T4) (the v equation has 4.1 v + 108).
# n counts the spikes and ts is the time of the last one, both set where the reset is (bounds is raised: ts is a time,
# which the default bound of 100 would stop).
par a = 0.02, b = -0.1, c = -55, d = 6
par I0 = 9, w = 2, T1 = 9.0909090909, T2 = 14.0909090909, T3 = 70, T4 = 80
init v = -60, u = 6, n = 0, ts = 0
I = I0*(heav(t-T1)-heav(t-T1-w))+I0*(heav(t-T2)-heav(t-T2-w))+I0*(heav(t-T3)-heav(t-T3-w))+I0*(heav(t-T4)-heav(t-T4-w))
v' = 0.04*v^2+4.1*v+108-u+I
u' = a*(b*v-u)
n' = 0
ts' = 0
event 1 v-30, v = c, u = u+d, n = n+1, ts = t
aux I = I
options {
  integration: { total_time: 100, dt: 0.025, store_every: 10, bound: 1000000 },
}
